;
INTRODUCTION TO CP/M

A - Finding your way around CP/M
B - The drives and named directories

:A
FINDING YOUR WAY AROUND CP/M

The CP/M prompt tells you where you are. On this system you may see a drive
and user area, or a name associated with that location. For example, A0: and
SYSTEM: refer to the same directory on a standard installation.

Unlike the directory tree of a modern computer, CP/M organizes files by drive
and numbered user area. The combination of the two is often called a DU. J3:
means drive J, user area 3. There are sixteen user areas per drive, numbered 0
through 15.

A command may refer to a file in another DU without moving you there. For
example:

    DIR J1:
    LT A0:WELCOME.TXT

To make a directory current, enter its DU or name followed by a colon:

    SIGM:
    DIR
    A0:

You can use upper or lower case for ordinary command and file names. Passwords
in RBBS are a separate matter and are case-sensitive.

CP/M filenames have up to eight characters, followed by an optional extension
of up to three characters. You'll often see names such as README.TXT,
PROGRAM.COM, GUIDE.DOC and SIGMV011.LBR.

Two useful filename wildcards are the question mark, which matches a
character, and the asterisk, which matches the remainder of a filename or
extension. Try:

    DIR P0:*.LBR
    DIR J1:*.DOC

Not every command accepts wildcards; use them where the command's instructions
permit. If you have been away from CP/M for a while, HELP CPM is a handy
reminder.

:B
THE DRIVES AND NAMED DIRECTORIES

A standard HUMONGOUS RCP/M has four drives. The sysop can customize their
contents and the access you have to them.

  A0: / SYSTEM:    Public commands and the welcome message
  I0: / WORK:      Temporary workspace for programs
  I14: / HELP:     Online HELP topics
  J:               The selected smaller software collection
  P:               The selected large software archive
  P0: / SIGM:      SIG/M libraries on the default P:
  P1: / CPMUG:     CPMUG libraries on the default P:

There are also private system and bulletin board areas. You cannot ordinarily
read or change their files. Selecting a DU or knowing its name does not confer
permission to use it.

J: holds a nominal 4 MB collection; P: is a 512 MiB archive disk. Either can
be exchanged using MOUNT. The SIGM and CPMUG names therefore describe the
default P: disk, not every disk that can be mounted there.

Named directories save a good deal of typing and remembering. If a volume
gives one of its user areas the name INFOCOM, for instance, you can enter that
directory by typing INFOCOM: rather than having to remember which J: user area
contains it.

The names are rebuilt automatically when you mount another volume. If a name
isn't present, use the numeric DU or look at the volume's guide instead.
